  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1133 PM CDT MON OCT 5 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals an upper trough swinging\nacross the eastern half of the country. At the surface, a cold\nfront can be analyzed draped across Central Alabama. Much drier\nair is beginning to filter in to the north of this boundary with\nPWATs already down to around 0.5\" per the GOES TPW imagery.\nShowers and thunderstorms can be seen ongoing to the south of the\nboundary, closer to the Gulf coast. This front will linger across\nthe southeast for several days before washing out. Surface high\npressure and weak ridging aloft will keep dry conditions in place\nthrough much of the work week. While days will still be warm with\nhighs in the mid to upper 80s, we will have a few cooler mornings\nwith lows dropping into the mid 50s for most.\n\nAll eyes turn to our southwest as we keep a close eye on a\ndisturbance over the southwestern Gulf. The NHC maintains a high\n(80%) chance of development over the next 7 days as this cluster\nof disorganized showers and thunderstorms drifts east-northeast\nacross the Gulf. A tropical depression is expected to form by the\nmiddle of the week. At this time, it's a bit too early to\nhighlight specific impacts across Central Alabama. However, we\ncan expect an increase in rain chances Friday through the\nweekend. The latest rainfall amounts are likely to range from 2-\n3\". A couple members of the LREF are hinting at a 20-30% chance\nof isolated amounts over 3\".\n\n95/Castillo",
